## Strategy
You are doing great! Moving from sorting simple integers to sorting an array of structs is a fantastic step forward. The logic remains exactly the same as the **Bubble Sort** we saw in `demo_problem_sort.c`. 

The secret is that instead of comparing two integers like `arr[j] > arr[j + 1]`, you simply compare the specific **field** inside your structs. If you have a struct representing a student with a `score` field, you just swap the entire struct when the `score` of one is greater than the other. You are essentially treating the struct as a single "unit" that moves together in memory!

